BOARD OVERVIEW
1. Executive Specification
A single view of what the company is building, why it matters, what already exists and what must be completed.
1.1 Product in one sentence
AuthClaw ITSM is a secure, AI-native, ITIL-aligned service management product that brings incidents, requests, changes, problems, knowledge, service configuration, workflows, approvals, analytics and governed AI execution into one tenant-isolated operating system for enterprise IT.
1.2 Objective
 Create one operational system of record for IT support and service delivery, replacing fragmented ticketing, manual coordination and disconnected evidence.
 Make self-service and automated fulfillment the default for repeatable services while preserving policy, approvals and human accountability.
 Reduce incident acknowledgement and resolution time by joining service context, knowledge, history, workflows and recommendations in the same workspace.
 Improve change success and reduce repeat incidents through a service graph, deterministic controls, evidence-backed analysis and governed automation.
 Extend AuthClaw from AI governance and compliance infrastructure into a full operational product, reusing the same identity, policy, gateway, audit, encryption and deployment foundations.
1.3 What the product is -- and is not
The product is
The product is not
A native ITSM product with incident, request, catalog, knowledge, SLA, problem, change, CMDB, on-call, workspace and analytics capabilities.
A thin chatbot placed on top of another ticketing system.
An API-first, workflow-driven, multi-tenant platform deployable as SaaS, private cloud or customer-hosted.
A promise to reproduce every adjacent module of the largest legacy ITSM suites.
An AI-assisted and eventually policy-bounded automation system with human gates, typed tools and complete decision traces.
An unrestricted autonomous agent with direct database, shell, cloud or provider credentials.
A continuation of AuthClaw's governance architecture and controls.
A disconnected codebase that duplicates AuthClaw identity, audit and security services.
1.4 Current truth

Board-level status statement
The current evidence shows a mature AuthClaw governance and compliance foundation, but the ITSM workbook marks all 135 ITSM capabilities as Planned. The company has created the detailed product definition, architecture, data model, workflows, permission model, security requirements, test gates and technology direction. It has not yet provided source evidence that the ITSM domain modules themselves are complete.

Evidence class
What exists or is defined
Available AuthClaw foundation
Go model gateway; FastAPI control plane; tenant identity/RBAC; OIDC; policy configuration; LangGraph agent service; redaction; HITL; immutable audit pipeline; PostgreSQL, Redis, Redpanda/Kafka and ClickHouse; Next.js console; Terraform-based AWS runtime; CI security/evidence gates.
Specified ITSM product
135 capabilities across 19 domains; 17 reference workflows; 18 role templates; canonical data model; 14 integration contracts; 10 AI agent/skill specifications; 27 security requirements; 24 NFR/SLO requirements; acceptance strategy and risk register.
Proposed additions
Durable workflow engine, ACL-aware search, vector retrieval, object storage and malware scanning, ITSM-specific domain modules, service graph/CMDB, notification adapters and operating telemetry.
STRATEGIC INTENT
2. Product Goal, Outcomes and Design Principles
The specification optimizes for trusted service operations, not feature count alone.
2.1 Primary product outcomes
Outcome
Meaning in the product
Unified operations
Every supported interaction becomes a governed record with a clear owner, state, service context, evidence trail and next action.
Faster restoration
Agents see history, related records, service impact, known errors, knowledge and suggested investigation steps without switching systems.
Safe change velocity
Change risk, conflicts, approvals, implementation evidence, rollback plans and post-implementation outcomes remain connected.
Repeat-work reduction
Recurring incidents lead to problems and known errors; repeatable requests lead to catalog workflows and bounded automation.
Transparent leadership view
Service performance, SLA compliance, change success, demand, backlog health, experience and AI outcomes use reproducible definitions.
Governed AI
AI may recommend, summarize and plan; actions are authorized by deterministic platform policy and verified after execution.
2.2 Non-negotiable design principles
API-first.  The web interface uses the same governed business services exposed through versioned APIs.
Policy before autonomy.  No AI or workflow can bypass identity, permissions, segregation of duties, approval or change controls.
Configuration over forks.  Forms, fields, taxonomies, SLAs, routing, notifications and supported workflows are versioned configuration -- not customer-specific branches.
Evidence by default.  Privileged changes, automated decisions, AI tool calls, approvals and policy outcomes are reconstructable.
Tenant isolation everywhere.  Identity, API, domain services, database, caches, queues, search, vector retrieval, object storage and analytics all enforce the same tenant boundary.
Human-centred automation.  Automation removes repetitive work but keeps understandable status, safe fallbacks, manual handoff and accountable ownership.
Independent deployability.  The ITSM product benefits from AuthClaw and an optional governed-memory adapter, but its core record and workflow functions remain independently operable.
2.3 Success measures
Dimension
Board-visible measure
Product interpretation
Service speed
Mean time to acknowledge; mean time to restore/resolve; median handling time
Whether the platform removes delay rather than simply recording it
Quality
First-contact resolution; reopen rate; repeat incident rate; problem remediation closure
Whether resolutions are durable and knowledge improves
Change safety
Change success, failed change, rollback and change-caused incident rates
Whether governance supports delivery instead of becoming bureaucracy
Self-service
Deflection, catalog completion, automation rate and abandonment
Whether employees can complete common outcomes without analyst intervention
Commitments
SLA/OLA compliance, breach risk, backlog age and queue health
Whether obligations are visible and actively managed
Experience
Requester satisfaction, agent effort and accessibility success
Whether the system is usable across both sides of service delivery
AI trust
Groundedness, override rate, task success, blocked unsafe actions and escalation quality
Whether AI is useful, explainable and governed
OPERATING FIT
3. Target Market and Use Context
This is product-fit analysis only; it is not a sales, marketing, pricing or channel strategy.
3.1 Primary operating profiles
 Mid-market and enterprise organizations that need a formal service desk, service catalog, change control, knowledge base and measurable service commitments.
 Regulated or security-conscious organizations that require tenant isolation, SSO, auditable access, encrypted secrets, retention controls and evidence-backed AI.
 Technology-led organizations seeking API-first integration with identity, collaboration, DevOps, cloud, monitoring and data platforms.
 Organizations constrained by rigid legacy ITSM implementations and customer-specific code forks, but still needing enterprise governance.
 Managed service or multi-business-unit environments that require delegated administration, scoped queues, domain separation and strong customer data boundaries.
 Data-sovereignty and deployment-sensitive environments requiring SaaS, private-cloud or customer-hosted choices from a single codebase.
3.2 Core user groups
User group
Primary need
Product experience
Employees / requesters
Get help, find answers, order approved services and understand status
Accessible portal, conversational assistance, catalog, knowledge and transparent progress
Service desk agents
Triage and resolve work with context and fewer handoffs
Unified workspace, queues, activity, related records, SLA risk and cited recommendations
Specialist resolvers / SRE
Investigate services, execute runbooks and coordinate changes
Service graph, diagnostics, work tasks, controlled tools and evidence capture
Managers and service owners
Control workload, commitments, quality and service health
Role dashboards, escalations, schedules, trends and drill-down
Process owners
Govern incident, problem, change, request and knowledge practices
Configurable lifecycle, policy, approvals, definitions and reporting
Platform / tenant administrators
Configure without creating upgrade forks
Versioned configuration, feature flags, forms, workflows, connectors and promotion controls
Security, risk and audit
Prove access, integrity, policy, data handling and AI accountability
Immutable evidence, negative authorization, exports, AI traces and control views
Developers and integrators
Automate and connect the platform safely
Versioned APIs, webhooks, SDKs, typed actions and connector framework
3.3 Deliberate market boundary
The native core covers IT service management. Deep observability ingestion and correlation, endpoint management, advanced software/hardware asset management, vulnerability response, security operations and broad enterprise-service-management templates are integration surfaces or future extensions -- not conditions for the core ITSM product to be valuable.
SCOPE
4. Product Scope and Boundaries
The core stays coherent by being explicit about native responsibilities, integrations and exclusions.
4.1 Native product scope
 Platform foundation: tenants, organizations, identity, permissions, forms, records, search, configuration, audit, retention and attachments.
 Incident and major-incident management, including intake, priority, routing, SLA, collaboration, communications and post-incident review.
 Problem management: clustering, root-cause analysis, known errors, corrective actions and recurrence trends.
 Change management: models, risk, conflicts, calendars, approvals, CAB, implementation, rollback evidence and review.
 Request management and service catalog: eligibility, variables, approvals, fulfillment, bundles, status and cancellation/compensation.
 Knowledge management: authoring, review, publishing, search, feedback, freshness and AI-assisted drafting.
 CMDB and service graph: configuration items, relationships, services, imports, reconciliation, health and impact context.
 SLAs and operational commitments: business calendars, pause rules, warnings, breach management and historical snapshots.
 Portal, agent workspace, queues, collaboration, on-call schedules, notifications, analytics, administration and developer interfaces.
 Governed AI skills for triage, investigation, planning, summarization, knowledge, assistance and bounded actions.
4.2 Integration or extension boundary
Area
Boundary decision
Monitoring / AIOps
Ingest normalized events and alerts; advanced cross-domain telemetry correlation remains a connector or later service-operations layer.
Endpoint management
Use connectors and approved actions; do not rebuild full device management in the initial native core.
Software and hardware asset management
Hold service-relevant CI and ownership context; advanced licensing, depreciation and procurement remain outside this document.
Security operations
Link security events/findings when useful, while dedicated case response and vulnerability programs remain separate products or integrations.
Enterprise service management
Reuse forms, tasks and workflows later through certified domain templates; do not dilute ITSM semantics in the core.
Long-term agent memory
Use native record/knowledge memory by default; optionally connect the governed memory component through a policy-scoped adapter.
4.3 Explicit exclusions from this specification
 Delivery dates and calendar schedule.
 Project budgets, operating costs, model pricing, licensing economics and financial forecasts.
 Sales positioning, marketing messages, campaigns, quotas, channels and commercial packaging.
 Public compliance claims or certification commitments.
 Customer-specific custom-code forks and unrestricted arbitrary-code execution.
