CAPABILITY SPECIFICATION
6. Detailed Product Capabilities
The following domains form one coherent service-management product, not a collection of unrelated screens.
6.1 Platform foundation
The shared record, identity, form, search, configuration and audit layer on which every ITSM module depends.
 Organizations, departments, locations, groups and delegated administrative domains with strict tenant isolation.
 Enterprise SSO, lifecycle provisioning, RBAC/ABAC, record/field/action permissions and segregation of duties.
 Metadata-driven forms with conditional visibility, validations, references, computed values and versioned schemas.
 Human-readable immutable record numbers, common task fields, activity streams, attachments, watchers and relationships.
 Global ACL-aware search, tenant settings, feature flags, retention, legal hold, export and verified purge.
 Append-only audit for identity, admin, business, integration, workflow and AI actions.
6.2 Incident management
Restore normal service quickly while maintaining ownership, communication, context and measurable commitments.
 Create incidents consistently from portal, agent UI, email, API, chat or monitoring sources.
 Calculate priority from impact and urgency with controlled, reasoned overrides.
 Classify by category, service and configuration item; route by skill, schedule, load, location, service and escalation policy.
 Guard state transitions, pending reasons, reopening, resolution evidence and requester communication.
 Link parents, children, duplicates, problems, changes, knowledge, services and CIs.
 Run response, assignment, restoration and resolution commitments across calendars, pauses, holidays and warnings.
6.3 Major incident management
Coordinate high-impact events through one command workspace and evidence-based communication process.
 Promote a candidate, assess impact and affected services, then assign incident commander and communications lead.
 Maintain war-room timeline, decisions, tasks, stakeholders, bridge/chat links and restoration checkpoints.
 Issue audience-controlled communications with cadence, approval and delivery status.
 Create post-incident review, problems and corrective actions from the verified timeline.
6.4 Problem management
Convert recurring symptoms into known causes, workarounds and permanent corrective actions.
 Create problems manually or from incident clusters, major incidents, analytics signals or APIs.
 Use a controlled lifecycle for assessment, RCA, known error, remediation, verification and closure.
 Provide 5-Whys/fishbone-style evidence workspaces, hypotheses, validation and service/CI links.
 Publish governed known errors and connect corrective actions to change/request/task records.
6.5 Change management
Increase successful change throughput without sacrificing accountability or service safety.
 Support normal, standard and emergency models with different evidence and control requirements.
 Calculate reproducible risk from criticality, timing, history, blast radius, test evidence and conflicts.
 Build deterministic serial, parallel, quorum and delegated approval paths with segregation of duties.
 Expose calendars, freezes, blackouts, overlapping changes, shared dependencies and on-call constraints.
 Provide CAB workbench, implementation tasks, checkpoints, evidence, rollback and post-implementation review.
 Connect authenticated CI/CD events to approved changes without shared human credentials.
6.6 Request management and service catalog
Turn standard employee needs into discoverable, eligible, governed and reusable fulfillment journeys.
 Represent an order as a request with one or more independently governed request items and fulfillment tasks.
 Expose only eligible catalog items based on organization, role, location, employment and service policy.
 Allow administrators to define variables, validation, approvals, workflow bindings, versions, previews and publication.
 Support manager, owner, security and data-owner approvals through serial, parallel and quorum patterns.
 Compose reusable human tasks, integrations, waits, validation and compensating actions.
 Give requesters honest progress without leaking internal work notes or restricted details.
6.7 Knowledge management
Capture validated operational knowledge and make it securely discoverable at the point of need.
 Draft, review, publish, expire and retire versioned articles by audience and taxonomy.
 Provide reusable templates for how-to, troubleshooting, known error, FAQ and policy content.
 Combine keyword, synonym, typo-tolerant and semantic search with pre-retrieval ACL enforcement.
 Measure feedback, reuse, deflection and staleness; notify owners and exclude expired content by default.
 Draft content from resolved work with citations and redaction, always requiring a human publish gate.
6.8 CMDB and service graph
Provide trusted configuration and service context for impact, risk, routing and AI grounding.
 Model configuration-item classes, inheritance, lifecycle, identifiers, criticality and ownership.
 Maintain typed, directional relationships such as depends-on, runs-on, hosted-on, connects-to and supports.
 Reconcile sources deterministically using identity rules, source precedence, attribute ownership and conflict visibility.
 Measure completeness, correctness, staleness, duplicates and orphan relationships.
 Support dry-run imports and connectors with checkpoints, retry, idempotency and source attribution.
 Represent business and technical services, offerings, consumers, owners, support groups, dependencies and operating hours.
6.9 SLA and commitments
Make service promises executable, visible and historically reproducible.
 Define response, assignment, restoration, resolution and fulfillment targets with conditions and business calendars.
 Run timers with deterministic start, pause, resume, stop and breach behavior across time zones and holidays.
 Warn before breach, escalate by policy and preserve definition versions so historic results do not change.
6.10 Self-service and portal
Make requesting help or services understandable, accessible and efficient.
 Personalized home, search, service catalog, knowledge, virtual assistance and status views.
 Guided forms, eligibility checks, attachment handling, draft preservation and clear validation errors.
 Requester-visible activity separated from internal notes and security-sensitive context.
 WCAG-aligned keyboard, screen-reader, focus, contrast and error handling in critical journeys.
6.11 Agent workspace
Give resolvers one work surface with queues, context, collaboration and safe actions.
 Role-scoped queues, saved filters, ownership, workload, priority and SLA-risk indicators.
 Single-record workspace combining fields, activity, service/CI graph, relationships, attachments, knowledge and workflow state.
 Quick actions and templates that invoke the same permission and validation services as full forms.
 Collaboration, swarm, handoff and cited AI summaries without cross-team data leakage.
6.12 Workflow and automation
Make long-running service processes durable, observable, versioned and safe under failure.
 Durable state, timers, approvals, human waits, retries, timeouts, idempotency and compensating actions.
 Visual configuration for supported triggers, conditions, actions, branches and subflows -- without arbitrary code.
 Deterministic business-rule ordering, execution trace and recursion guard.
 Scheduled jobs with time-zone handling, concurrency policy, missed-run behavior and history.
 Typed reusable actions and a policy-aware dry run showing proposed side effects before execution.
6.13 Notifications and collaboration
Deliver the right message to the right audience without duplication or information leakage.
 Thread-safe inbound/outbound email with message correlation, retry, secure attachments and sender identity rules.
 Rule-driven email, SMS, chat and web notifications with templates, localization, throttling, digest and delivery trace.
 Tenant-bound collaboration integrations for support, approvals, updates and deep links using least-privilege OAuth.
6.14 On-call management
Identify and escalate to the correct responder across schedules, time zones and handoffs.
 Primary/secondary rotations, overrides, holidays and follow-the-sun schedules.
 Sequential or parallel escalation, acknowledgement timeout, retry and multi-channel notification.
 Evidence-backed handoff summaries of open incidents, major incidents, risky changes and commitment risks.
6.15 Analytics and reporting
Create reproducible operational insight without destabilizing the transactional system.
 Role dashboards for service desk, process managers, service owners, CMDB, executives and security.
 Documented definitions for MTTA, MTTR, FCR, reopen, backlog age, SLA, satisfaction, deflection, change success and repeat incidents.
 Historical metric snapshots that do not change when current records are edited later.
 Permission-aware ad hoc analysis, filters, grouping, charts, drill-down and scheduled export.
6.16 Integration platform
Connect enterprise systems through secure, replayable and supportable contracts.
 Versioned REST APIs, signed webhooks, internal domain events and generated client SDKs.
 Connector framework with OAuth/workload identity, vault references, mapping, checkpoints, retry and health status.
 Migration tools for users, groups, work records, knowledge, CIs and attachments with dry run and reconciliation.
 No secret values returned to browsers, logs, tickets, prompts or exports.
6.17 AI and agentic capabilities
Apply AI where it improves decisions and execution while keeping the platform -- not the model -- in control.
 Provider-neutral gateway, tenant model allowlists, redaction, retention policy and fallback.
 Permission-aware RAG with source citations and no post-filter-only leakage design.
 Triage, investigation, plan drafting, conflict explanation, RCA support, knowledge drafting, summaries and requester assistance.
 Typed tools, precheck, authorization, human gate, idempotent execution, postcheck, rollback and complete trace.
 Versioned evaluation sets for groundedness, task success, leakage, tool correctness, regression, latency and safety.
6.18 Administration
Enable safe tenant-level configuration with versioning, audit and rollback.
 One console for organizations, roles, forms, taxonomy, workflows, SLAs, notifications, integrations, AI policy and feature flags.
 Signed configuration transport with dependency checks, diffs and rollback; secrets never move inside configuration bundles.
6.19 Developer platform
Offer supported extension paths while protecting the core runtime and upgradeability.
 Generated TypeScript and Python API clients with authentication, pagination, retries and idempotency helpers.
 Supported webhooks, namespaced custom fields/tables, UI slots, typed workflow actions and connector SDK.
 No extension may mutate the core schema or execute privileged arbitrary code outside policy.
USER JOURNEYS
7. End-to-End Operating Journeys
The product is complete only when records, policy, workflow, communication, evidence and analytics work together.
7.1 Incident restoration
 A requester, email, API, chat or monitoring source creates a canonical incident. Source identity and tenant are verified; unsafe content or attachments are quarantined.
 Rules and optional AI suggest category, service, CI, priority and assignment. Deterministic policy selects what may be applied automatically; the evidence and confidence remain visible.
 The incident enters the correct queue, commitments start, owners are notified and the requester sees a safe status.
 The resolver investigates using activity, service graph, related incidents, changes, known errors and knowledge. Read-only diagnostics or proposed runbooks pass through typed tools.
 A high-impact case becomes a major-incident candidate. Authorized users declare it, assign command roles and initiate communications.
 Resolution requires structured evidence. The requester is notified, reopen policy applies, outcome metrics update and a knowledge/problem candidate may be proposed.
7.2 Service request fulfillment
 The requester searches the catalog or uses conversation. The server checks identity, eligibility and mandatory variables before presenting an order summary.
 The platform creates a request and one or more items using an idempotency key so duplicate submissions do not create duplicate work.
 Approval policy calculates the required path from current context and segregation-of-duties rules.
 A durable workflow performs human tasks, integrations, waits and verification. Failure leads to retry, compensation or manual intervention according to the action definition.
 The requester sees meaningful progress without internal notes; completed actions, approvals and evidence remain linked to the item.
7.3 Controlled change
 An authorized initiator creates a change manually or from an authenticated delivery pipeline.
 The product validates implementation, test, rollback and verification plans, then calculates reproducible risk and deterministic conflicts from service graph and calendars.
 Approval and CAB policy apply by risk, service, environment and emergency status; the initiator cannot approve where segregation rules prohibit it.
 Implementation tasks execute only inside the authorized window. Evidence, checkpoints and unexpected conditions are recorded; rollback or incident creation is available.
 The post-implementation review records success, failure, incidents, rollback and lessons, feeding future risk and analytics.
7.4 AI-assisted bounded action
 A user or workflow submits a goal with tenant, identity, record, allowed context and expiry.
 The agent retrieves only authorized evidence, labels untrusted content and creates a structured plan using registered tools.
 The platform policy evaluates identity, role, data class, target, environment, action risk, separation of duties, required approval and limits.
 An authorized human approves when required. The tool gateway resolves credentials by reference; the model never receives them.
 Execution is idempotent. Postchecks verify the target state; a failed postcheck triggers compensation, rollback or escalation.
 The final result includes evidence, policy decision, approvals, tool inputs/outputs, verification and unresolved questions.
AI AND MEMORY
8. AI-Native Operating Model
AI is embedded in product workflows, but authority remains with the deterministic platform.
8.1 AI capability ladder
Level
Allowed behavior
Examples
