OIDC / SAML
Tenant context
RBAC / quotas
ITSM domain services
Rules
Durable workflows
Workers
Go model gateway
OPA policy
LangGraph agents
Typed tools
PostgreSQL
Search/vector
Object store
Kafka/ClickHouse


Core architectural rule
The transactional ITSM services own business state. The workflow engine owns durable orchestration state. The model gateway owns model egress. The policy service owns authorization decisions. The audit pipeline owns tamper-resistant evidence. No component silently assumes another component's authority.
9.2 Current AuthClaw technology reused directly
Layer
Current technology
Reuse decision
Web console
Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind, Shadcn/Radix, TanStack Query, Zustand, Recharts
Extend into requester, agent, manager and admin experiences using the same component and authentication patterns.
Control plane / APIs
Python, FastAPI, Pydantic, SQLAlchemy, Alembic, PostgreSQL
Keep tenant, identity, configuration and policy administration; add ITSM modules behind stable service boundaries.
Model gateway
Go, chi router, PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka and provider adapters
Retain as the only supported AI-provider egress and enforcement path.
Agent services
Python, LangGraph, provider adapters, Presidio, document-processing libraries
Retain as a separate process and add ITSM skill graphs and typed tools.
Policy / sensitive data
OPA/Rego, YAML policies, Microsoft Presidio plus specialized detection
Use one decision model across API, workflow and AI; redact before model egress and sensitive telemetry.
Transactional storage
PostgreSQL 17, RLS defense in depth, encrypted fields and Alembic migrations
Use as system of record with tenant key on each tenant-owned table.
Cache / ephemeral state
Redis 7
Use tenant-scoped keys for sessions, quotas, short-lived state and safe caches; never as authoritative record state.
Events and audit
Redpanda/Kafka, ClickHouse, hash chains, signed export and PostgreSQL fallback
Expand event schemas for ITSM records, workflows, commitments and AI actions.
Infrastructure
Containers; Terraform on AWS using VPC, ALB, ECS, RDS, ElastiCache, KMS, Secrets Manager, CloudWatch and Route53 failover
Use environment profiles and extend for ITSM stores/services; maintain a single codebase across deployment modes.
Quality/security pipeline
Pytest, Go tests, Playwright, contract tests, gitleaks-style secrets gates, SAST/SCA/IaC/container checks and evidence validators
Add domain, workflow durability, search security, accessibility, migration and AI evaluation suites.
9.3 New or expanded components required for ITSM
Component
Preferred direction
Reason / control objective
ITSM domain runtime
Modular FastAPI services initially; separate only when scale or ownership requires
Reuse AuthClaw patterns while preserving incident, request, change, problem, knowledge, SLA and CMDB boundaries.
Durable workflow engine
Temporal- or Camunda-style mature orchestration
Human waits, timers, retry, replay and idempotency must survive process failure; do not build custom durability.
Search
OpenSearch/Elasticsearch-compatible full-text and faceting
ACL-aware global search, knowledge relevance, synonyms and rebuildable derived indexes.
Vector retrieval
pgvector or managed search vector fields
Permission-aware RAG with tenant namespaces and source pointers.
Object storage
S3/Blob/GCS-compatible with signed URLs
Attachments, imports, exports and evidence packages require encrypted, scanned and lifecycle-managed binary storage.
Notifications
Provider adapters for email, chat, SMS and web
Templates, retry, throttling, delivery status and isolation from internal notes.
Observability
OpenTelemetry with Prometheus/Grafana or managed equivalents
Correlate UI, API, workflow, events and AI without logging sensitive bodies.
9.4 Service boundaries
 Identity and tenant authorization remain authoritative in the control plane.
 ITSM domain modules validate business state and emit versioned domain events after committed changes.
 Workflow definitions reference domain actions; workflows do not mutate database tables directly.
 Search, vector and analytics are derived stores and can be rebuilt from authoritative records/events.
 High-volume or high-risk workers run in separate pools so one connector or import cannot starve incident operations.
 Every request propagates tenant, actor, correlation and trace identifiers across synchronous and asynchronous boundaries.
DATA
10. Data Architecture, Ownership and Lifecycle
The product treats operational data, derived indexes, AI context and audit evidence differently.
10.1 Systems of record
Data class
Authority
Examples
Key rule
Transactional domain data
PostgreSQL service-owned schema
Incidents, requests, changes, problems, knowledge metadata, approvals, CIs, services, SLAs
Strong validation, optimistic concurrency, tenant key and versioned migrations
Workflow state
Durable workflow engine
Timers, waits, current step, retries, compensation
Replayable and idempotent; business record stores status references, not engine internals
Binary objects
Encrypted object storage
Attachments, imports, exports, evidence packages
Metadata and authorization in database; signed time-bound access; scanning/quarantine
Search and vector indexes
Derived and rebuildable
Full text, facets, embeddings and source pointers
Pre-retrieval ACL/tenant filtering; never sole source of truth
Audit evidence
Append-only audit store plus immutable export
Access, changes, approvals, AI/tool traces
Restricted writers/readers, integrity verification and independent retention
Analytics facts
Snapshot/read model, ClickHouse at scale
Metric periods, lifecycle events and trends
Definitions are versioned; heavy queries do not burden transactional services
10.2 Tenant isolation strategy
 Tenant comes from authenticated context, never solely from a request body.
 Every tenant-owned table, object prefix, cache key, queue message, search document, vector namespace and analytics fact carries tenant ownership.
 Application repository filters are mandatory; PostgreSQL row-level security provides defense in depth.
 Search and retrieval filter before ranking or model context construction; post-filter-only security is prohibited.
 Cross-tenant negative tests cover guessed identifiers, caches, exports, events, object URLs, semantic retrieval and administrative support access.
10.3 Data lifecycle
Stage
Required behavior
Create / ingest
Validate schema and identity; classify sensitivity; scan files; record source; apply tenant and retention metadata.
Use
Enforce record, field and action permissions; mask sensitive fields; log references and decision metadata rather than unrestricted bodies.
Derive
Index or embed only authorized content with source pointers, ACL metadata, model/version provenance and refresh status.
Share / export
Require scoped authorization and step-up where appropriate; use encrypted expiring delivery and integrity manifests.
Retain / hold
Apply record-specific retention; preserve version/audit evidence; legal hold blocks deletion.
Delete
Reconcile purge across database, objects, search, vector, cache and derivative stores while retaining only lawful minimal audit evidence.
SECURITY
11. Security, Privacy and Compliance by Design
Security controls are product behavior and acceptance gates, not later hardening.
11.1 Security model
Control plane
Required behavior
Identity
Enterprise OIDC/SAML, optional lifecycle provisioning, secure sessions, MFA/step-up and service/workload identities.
Authorization
Deny-by-default RBAC/ABAC with record, field and action scope; segregation of duties; delegated administration; one policy model for API, workflow and AI.
Secrets
Vault or cloud secret manager; workload identity; encrypted references; values never returned after save or placed in prompts, tickets, logs or configuration bundles.
Encryption
TLS in transit; managed storage and backup encryption; envelope encryption for sensitive fields and connector credentials; key versioning and rotation.
Application security
Schema validation, parameterized access, CSRF/XSS/SSRF protections, WAF, file scanning, dependency and container scanning.
Privileged access
Just-in-time or break-glass access with SSO, MFA, reason, time limit, session audit and independent review.
AI security
Untrusted-content separation, ACL retrieval, model/provider allowlists, tool schema validation, action policy, egress controls and adversarial evaluation.
Audit integrity
Append-only events with actor, target, action, time, tenant, correlation, policy, approval and before/after summary; hash verification and signed export.
11.2 Shared-responsibility boundary
The product can implement technical controls and generate evidence supporting GDPR obligations, SOC 2 readiness and security governance. It cannot by itself create an organization's legal basis, policies, training, vendor agreements, access reviews, incident response discipline, backup tests or independent audit report. The board must treat compliance claims as evidence-based and externally reviewed.
11.3 Truthful compliance posture

Internal claim boundary
AuthClaw and the ITSM product may be described internally as designed with encryption, SSO, tenant isolation, audit, privacy and SOC 2 control evidence. They must not be represented as independently certified or as having completed a Type II examination unless the relevant external report exists. GDPR readiness also requires legal and operational review beyond product controls.
11.4 Secure development and release evidence
 Code review, protected branches, automated tests, dependency/security scanning, secret detection, infrastructure review and immutable artifact promotion.
 Threat models for tenant isolation, inbound email, attachments, connectors, search/vector leakage, AI prompt injection and tool abuse.
 Independent penetration testing covering web, API, identity, tenant and agentic-action boundaries before external production claims.
 Backup restore, failover, key rotation, incident response and data-subject workflow exercises with captured evidence.
 Software bill of materials, signed images/artifacts and provenance for release builds.
ACCESS
12. Identity, Roles and Permission Model
Permissions are atomic and scoped; role names are templates, not hard-coded privilege bundles.
12.1 Identity types
Identity
Purpose
Control
Human user
Requester, agent, manager, administrator, security or audit activity
SSO/session, tenant binding, role/group scope, MFA for sensitive actions
Service / workload
API client, connector, pipeline, worker or internal service
OAuth/workload identity, explicit scopes, rotation and no shared human credentials
Agent execution
Temporary delegated AI task
Actor plus task identity, policy envelope, allowed tools/context, limits and expiry
Support operator
Operate hosted service
No routine tenant-content access; break-glass, reason, time bound and audit when required
12.2 Authorization dimensions
 Tenant and administrative domain
 Role and group
 Record type and ownership
 Service, CI, queue or organizational scope
 Field visibility and mutation
 Action and lifecycle transition
 Environment and target
 Data classification
 Segregation of duties
 AI use case, tool and autonomy tier
12.3 Decision behavior
 Default deny; allow must be explicit and explainable.
 UI visibility improves experience but is never the only enforcement layer.
 The same policy semantics apply when an API, workflow, connector or AI agent requests an action.
 Permissions are re-evaluated at execution time, not inherited from an earlier plan or stale approval.
 Negative authorization events are audited with enough context to investigate without leaking protected data.
INTERFACES
13. APIs, Events and Integrations
Every external and internal contract is tenant-bound, versioned, observable and safe to replay.
13.1 API standards
 Versioned OpenAPI contracts, resource-oriented endpoints and explicit action endpoints for guarded transitions.
 Idempotency keys for create and action requests; event IDs for asynchronous deduplication.
 Optimistic concurrency through versions or ETags so critical updates do not silently overwrite one another.
 Pagination, filtering, sparse fields, consistent error envelopes and correlation identifiers.
 Server-side eligibility, permissions and transition validation; crafted payloads cannot bypass policy.
 Rate, concurrency, body-size and resource-consumption safeguards per tenant, identity and integration.
13.2 Event standards
 Versioned CloudEvents-like envelopes with event ID, tenant, actor/service, subject, type, time, correlation and schema version.
 At-least-once delivery with idempotent consumers, retry, dead-letter queues, replay and lag monitoring.
 Events carry minimum necessary data or protected references; they are not uncontrolled copies of record bodies.
 Breaking schema change requires version transition or dual publishing; additive evolution is the default.
13.3 Connector families
Connector family
Core use
Identity / directory
User and group provisioning, deprovisioning and approved access actions using SCIM/OAuth.
Email and collaboration
Incident/request intake, updates, approvals, notifications and deep links with tenant/workspace binding.
DevOps
Authenticated build, pull-request, deployment and change evidence; incident linkage.
Monitoring / event
Normalized signal intake, deduplication and correlation hooks.
Cloud and infrastructure
Read context and execute only approved typed runbooks through tenant-specific connections.
Vault
Resolve secrets by reference at execution time; log access, not value.
SIEM and audit
Stream normalized security/audit events with buffering and replay.
Migration / export
Checkpointed import and reconstruction-ready export with row-level results and integrity manifest.
OPERATIONS
14. Deployment, Reliability and Observability
The same product code supports different deployment profiles without customer-specific forks.
14.1 Deployment profiles
Profile
Characteristics
Control objective
Multi-tenant SaaS
Shared control plane and horizontally scaled services with strict tenant boundaries
Operational efficiency with isolation at every layer
Dedicated / private cloud
Customer- or region-dedicated runtime using the same containers and configuration model
Residency, isolation and regulated deployment needs
Customer-hosted
Documented container/Kubernetes or managed-container profile with conformance tests
No control regression and no product code fork
Development / test
Docker Compose with local PostgreSQL, Redis, OPA, Presidio, Redpanda, ClickHouse and mock model provider
Repeatable local and CI integration testing without real credentials
14.2 Reliability behavior
 Stateless web/API/gateway replicas scale horizontally; stateful services use managed replication, backup and point-in-time recovery.
 Core record operations degrade independently from AI, semantic search, reporting and external connectors.
 Workflow state, timers and approvals survive worker or node restart; actions are idempotent and compensatable.
 Queues and event buses use retry, dead-letter handling, backpressure and visibility into stuck work.
 Search and vector indexes are rebuildable; object, database and audit recovery are explicitly verified.
 Regional recovery proceeds in dependency order: identity/secrets, authoritative data, APIs, experiences, workflows and derived services.
14.3 Observability
Signal
Required content
Sensitive-data rule
Logs
Structured event, severity, service, tenant reference, correlation and outcome
No credentials, unrestricted record bodies or prompt content
Metrics
Traffic, errors, latency, saturation, queue lag, workflow age, connector health and product KPIs
Aggregate; avoid high-cardinality PII
Traces
UI-->API-->workflow-->event-->AI/tool spans and dependency timing
Use identifiers and redacted attributes
Audit
Security/business accountability and reconstructable decisions
Separate restricted access and retention from routine telemetry
SLO alerts
Availability, performance, durability, freshness, completeness and AI safety gates
Alerts link to evidence; do not copy sensitive content
QUALITY
15. Non-Functional Requirements and Acceptance
Product completeness includes speed, durability, accessibility, safety and supportability.
15.1 Key internal engineering targets
Area
Target / invariant
Degradation behavior
Core availability
Internal core API objective at or above 99.9% monthly
Safe degraded or read-only mode where technically sound
Core performance
p95 core read at or below 500 ms; standard mutation at or below 800 ms, excluding async work
Move enrichment and long work to queues
Portal experience
p75 largest-contentful-paint at or below 2.5 seconds on representative broadband
Defer noncritical widgets
Search
p95 keyword at or below 1.5 seconds; semantic at or below 2.5 seconds
Fall back from semantic to secure keyword search
Workflow durability
No lost timer or workflow state after process/node restart
Replay durable state and avoid duplicate side effects
Audit completeness
All privileged, administrative and AI tool actions have actor, time, target and context identifiers
Sensitive action may fail closed when evidence cannot be written
Accessibility
WCAG 2.2 AA-aligned core requester and agent journeys
Critical inaccessible functionality blocks acceptance
AI response
Interactive AI p95 at or below 8 seconds; longer tasks asynchronous with progress
Approved alternate model, deterministic path or human handoff
AI safety
No tolerated successful critical privilege escalation in the adversarial release set
Disable affected use case, model or tool
15.2 Test strategy
Test layer
What it proves
Unit and property tests
Domain rules, SLA time arithmetic, transition guards, reconciliation and validation are deterministic.
API and event contracts
Schemas, compatibility, idempotency, authentication and consumer/provider behavior stay stable.
Authorization and isolation
No cross-tenant, hidden-field, approval, search, export or agentic privilege bypass.
Workflow fault injection
Crash, retry, duplicate event, timeout and human wait do not lose state or repeat side effects.
End-to-end journeys
Portal/email/API intake through workflow, communication, resolution and evidence behave as one product.
Search and AI evaluations
Relevance, groundedness, leakage, citations, tool correctness, regressions and safe refusal meet thresholds.
Performance / resilience / recovery
Load, dependency failures, backup restore and regional recovery preserve correctness and target behavior.
Accessibility and compatibility
Keyboard, screen reader, focus, contrast, errors and supported browser journeys work.
Independent security testing
Web, API, auth, tenant and agentic boundaries withstand external exploit testing.
15.3 Definition of done for a product capability
 Business behavior and failure behavior are specified and tested.
 UI, API, workflow, event and permission semantics agree.
 Tenant isolation, field/action permissions and audit evidence are covered by negative tests.
 Accessibility, observability, support diagnostics, documentation and migration/rollback are present.
 AI-enabled capability has a versioned evaluation set, safe fallback, trace and human control appropriate to risk.
 No critical security or data-integrity defect remains open for the capability's intended use.
