RISK
17. Principal Risks, Dependencies and Mitigations
The biggest risks come from scope, workflow correctness, data isolation, service context and privileged automation.
Risk
Why it matters
Mitigation
Attempting legacy-suite breadth too early
Dilutes the native ITSM core and delays usable end-to-end journeys
Hold the native/integration boundary; validate incident, request and change depth before adjacent domains.
Over-generalized platform abstractions
Framework work can grow without proving a real process
Validate each abstraction against incident, request and change workflows before making it generic.
Tenant isolation gaps
Search, cache, events, vector retrieval or exports can leak data even when UI filtering is correct
Central policy, tenant-aware keys/indexes and adversarial cross-tenant tests at every layer.
Weak workflow durability
Duplicate or lost approvals/actions undermine service and audit integrity
Use a mature engine; idempotent actions; fault injection; replay and compensation evidence.
Poor CMDB quality
Impact, risk, routing and AI recommendations become misleading
Source attribution, reconciliation, confidence, health metrics and visible stale-data warnings.
AI presented as fact
False certainty can create operational or governance harm
Citations, evidence/confidence, safe refusal, human gates and use-case evaluation.
Agent tools become a privileged bypass
Automation could evade ordinary roles and change controls
Typed tool gateway, reauthorization, segregation of duties, approval, vault references and postcheck.
Customer-specific code forks
Upgrades and security posture diverge
Configuration-first product and stable, namespaced extension SDK with conformance tests.
SLA calculation defects
Service commitments and escalations become untrustworthy
Deterministic time engine with daylight-saving, holiday, pause and boundary tests.
Email/chat spoofing
Untrusted senders could update privileged records
Strong channel identity, correlation, OAuth/signed pipelines and limited unauthenticated behavior.
Reporting pressure on transactional data
Dashboards can harm service operations
Snapshots/read models and ClickHouse/warehouse path for heavy analytics.
Deployment-profile forks
Private deployments can become unsupported products
One codebase, environment profiles, signed configuration and conformance testing.
BOARD DECISIONS
18. Decisions and Questions Requiring Governance
These are product-shaping choices -- not schedule or budget decisions.
1
Product boundary
Confirm that deep AIOps, endpoint management, advanced asset management and SecOps remain integrations/extensions rather than core-native requirements.
2
Workflow standard
Approve the principle of adopting a mature durable workflow engine rather than building custom orchestration durability.
3
Deployment promise
Confirm which profiles are strategic commitments: multi-tenant SaaS, dedicated/private cloud and customer-hosted.
4
AI autonomy posture
Approve the capability ladder and require policy-bounded, typed, reversible execution; prohibit unrestricted autonomy.
5
Memory posture
Approve native record/knowledge memory as authoritative and an optional governed-memory adapter for cross-session use.
6
Technology consistency
Confirm that AuthClaw identity, gateway, policy, agent, audit, encryption, console and infrastructure patterns remain the shared platform.
7
Data and compliance boundary
Approve evidence-based internal claims and require independent legal/audit review before external certification language.
8
Configuration policy
Confirm configuration-first delivery and prohibit customer-specific core-code forks except through governed product extensions.
CONCLUSION
19. Board-Level Product Conclusion
AuthClaw ITSM is a logical portfolio extension because the hardest governance foundations already exist.
The product thesis is coherent: combine a full ITSM operating model with AuthClaw's existing identity, policy, model gateway, agent, sensitive-data protection, audit and deployment foundation. This allows AI to be part of service operations without becoming an ungoverned side channel.
The specification is intentionally deeper than a board overview because the key value -- and the key risk -- lies in how the parts join: records with workflows, workflows with permissions, AI with evidence, tools with approval, data with retention, and every action with audit.
The current product plan is comprehensive, but the implementation status must stay candid. AuthClaw's shared platform is real and reusable; the ITSM product remains a defined build until domain modules and end-to-end acceptance evidence exist. The next governance task is to approve the scope boundaries, architecture invariants, autonomy posture and shared-platform decisions contained in this document.

Recommended board interpretation
Approve the product definition and architectural invariants as the internal north star. Treat the appendix as the source-controlled scope baseline. Require future progress reporting to show evidence by capability: implemented, integrated, security-tested, operationally proven and accepted -- without substituting activity or feature count for product readiness.

